    "content": {
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Comparing and ordering integers"
        },
        {
          "type": "subheading",
          "text": "The number line"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "A number line shows integers in order. Zero is in the middle. Positive integers go right. Negative integers go left."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Values increase as you move right, so -7 < -2 < 0 < 4. Compare by position, not by digit size. -7 is smaller than -2 because it is farther left."
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "image_asset": "lesson/lesson-1/integer-number-line.png",
          "image_alt": "Number line from -8 to 8 showing integers in order",
          "caption": "Integers increase from left to right."
        },
        {
          "type": "video",
          "video_code": "VID-INTEGER-NUMBER-LINE",
          "segment_start_seconds": 60,
          "segment_end_seconds": 110
        },
        {
          "type": "worked_example",
          "title": "Ordering integers",
          "problem": "Order 4, -6, 0, and -1 from least to greatest.",
          "steps": [
            "Step 1: Place each number on the number line. -6 is farthest left and 4 is farthest right.",
            "Step 2: Between them, -1 is left of 0.",
            "Step 3: Read the numbers from left to right."
          ],
          "answer": "-6, -1, 0, 4"
        },
        {
          "type": "common_mistake",
          "text": "-7 > -3 is wrong. The digit 7 is bigger, but -7 is farther left. Correct: -7 < -3."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "variant": "cta",
          "text": "Ready to practice. Continue to the regular exercise for 6 questions."
        }
      ],
      "source_text": "Lesson 1.2 - Comparing and ordering integers\nSkill: S5\nObjective:\n1. Represent, compare, and order integers on a number line.\nThe number line\nA number line shows integers in order. Zero is in the middle. Positive integers go right. Negative integers go left.\nValues increase as you move right. So -7 < -2 < 0 < 4.\nCompare by position, not by digit size. -7 is smaller than -2 because it is farther left.\nWorked example\nProblem: Order 4, -6, 0, and -1 from least to greatest.\nStep 1: Place each number on the number line. -6 is farthest left and 4 is farthest right.\nStep 2: Between them, -1 is left of 0.\nStep 3: Read the numbers from left to right.\nAnswer: -6, -1, 0, 4.\nCommon mistake\n-7 > -3 is wrong. The digit 7 is bigger, but -7 is farther left. Correct: -7 < -3."
    }
  },
